Better: "How we would know I was right" — a prospective measure: before the run, each node's five fields state whether the tool was run, whether the fact is sourced, and where the piece's 勢 (check) is; after the run, count the failures that trace to a factor my three questions would have surfaced, and the tokens spent on nodes that should have been unclear spots. If the design's unclear-spot list at 3.3 grew by at least one item per challenge the method itself called "clear," and no dispatched node built on an unproven tool or an unsourced design decision, the help is real. Falsifier: if the questions are always answered "nothing missing," or the extra analysis costs more than the failures it catches, I was not reliable there.

